

@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Theming}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Colours}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Theming_Name @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Colours</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Theming_Name @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Colours</h1>

<p>
    The primary, neutral, secondary and accent colours are all derived from a root variable <span class="code-span">@CoreCssVariables.Colour_Primary</span>.
    This single root variable is then used to create the roots for neutral, secondary and accent colours which are then used to create the respective palettes.
    The primary and neutral variables that end "-50" are the same as the root colour that were used to create the lighter and darker shades.
</p>
<p>
    <b>For most applications you will most likely only need to change the single <span class="code-span">@CoreCssVariables.Colour_Primary</span> variable value.</b> But if the others are
    not to your liking then you just change them at the hierarchy level that best suits your application (see theming overview). And then use the colours that your root variables have
    generated.
</p>
<p>
    The success, warning, info and danger palettes are all created from their respective root variable which are fixed values (unless you change them) and thus not affected by
    the primary colour.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Note:</strong> The focus indicator in this library is a ring around the item and the colour used for the indicator variable
    <span class="code-span">--br-comp-all-focus-indicator-colour</span> is the primary derived value: <span class="code-span">@CoreCssVariables.Colour_Primary_70</span>
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Important: </strong> Although difficult given you are on this site, you must remember, things such as the left hand side background colour, the band at the top,
    all the heading colours (that are not from a component) are just things I picked for the site, these are not driven by the components so they would be your colours etc. 
    The components themselves, use a minimal set of colours, so once you assign your primary colour, you should have a good base, from which to add other splashes of colour 
    for your branding etc.
</p>
<p>
    All of the colour changes are driven by pure css colour functions so there are no css pre-processors or calls to some API - everything is in the Core project style sheet.
    For more advanced colour control over the generated neutrals, there is also a <span class="code-span">@CoreCssVariables.Colour_Neutral_Saturation</span> variable. I set this
    with a value of 10. If you want less of the primary tinge in the neutrals, just lower this value or raise it for a more.
</p>
<p>
    The browsers default colour picker below is bound to the primary colour, so just change it to change the entire site so you can go look at any component and use the
    dark theme switch at the top of the screen to see what everything looks like. Please note, there is no function that automatically picks the dark theme values, I just selected
    them but I think these would be satisfy a lot of applications. You can see what I used on the Theming overview page and I will be adding an export/copy to clipboard function on
    the sandbox page (once completed) for you to get all the variables for your sites style sheet.
</p>
<p>
    The neutral tinge slider below only goes from 0 to 15 (the library default is 10), but as the underlying CSS colour function uses a percentage you could go higher but you really then would not have neutrals.
    Don't forget to visit the Theming Accessibility page to check contrast ratios after changing the primary colour.
</p>
<p>
    <b>Note: </b> I often change the primary colour of this site and the test site from the default. Also note given that I have to get the computed
    css values (given the css colour functions), apply them to a canvas, convert them to hex/RGB for my colour contrast utility
    there may be slight rounding differences to other checkers by about 0.01 on some colours.
</p>

<ThemeTool />

